"""The fixed offline evaluation set for the study tutor: 25 IT-degree topics, each with a short source passage written
here (the tutor explains ONLY from the student's own material, so the harness needs material).  Synthetic notes, not the
owner's.  `NOTES[topic]` is the passage text; `AREAS` groups the topics; `WORKED` names the topics whose board must carry
a worked example with real numbers."""

AREAS = {
    "networking": ["Subnetting", "OSI model", "TCP three-way handshake", "DNS", "DHCP", "NAT", "VLANs"],
    "security": ["Hashing vs encryption", "Public-key cryptography", "Firewalls", "Phishing", "Least privilege"],
    "systems": ["Processes vs threads", "Virtual memory", "RAID", "File permissions", "Virtualization vs containers"],
    "databases": ["Database normalization", "Primary and foreign keys", "Database indexes", "Database transactions"],
    "programming": ["Recursion", "Big-O notation", "Pointers and references", "APIs and REST"],
}
TOPICS = [t for group in AREAS.values() for t in group]
WORKED = {"Subnetting", "Big-O notation", "RAID", "File permissions", "Recursion"}        # the board needs real numbers

NOTES = {
    "Subnetting": "Subnetting divides one IP network into smaller networks by borrowing bits from the host part of the address and giving them to the network part. The subnet mask or prefix length says how many bits are the network part: a /24 has 24 network bits and 8 host bits, so 2^8 = 256 addresses, of which 254 are usable because the first address is the network address and the last is the broadcast address. Borrowing 2 bits from a /24 gives four /26 subnets of 64 addresses each, which start at .0, .64, .128 and .192. Subnetting reduces broadcast traffic and lets an administrator separate departments.",
    "OSI model": "The OSI model describes network communication as seven layers: 7 Application, 6 Presentation, 5 Session, 4 Transport, 3 Network, 2 Data Link and 1 Physical. Each layer uses the services of the layer below and serves the layer above. On the sending side data moves down the stack and each layer adds its own header, which is called encapsulation; the receiver removes the headers going back up. IP works at layer 3, Ethernet switches at layer 2, and TCP and UDP at layer 4.",
    "TCP three-way handshake": "TCP sets up a connection with a three-way handshake before any data is sent. The client sends a SYN segment with its initial sequence number. The server answers with a SYN-ACK, which acknowledges the client's number and carries the server's own initial sequence number. The client replies with an ACK and the connection is established. The handshake lets both sides agree on sequence numbers so that lost or reordered segments can be detected.",
    "DNS": "The Domain Name System translates human-readable names such as example.com into IP addresses. A client asks a recursive resolver, which on a cache miss queries a root server, then a top-level domain server such as the one for .com, then the authoritative server for the domain, and returns the answer to the client. Answers are cached for the time given in the record's TTL. An A record maps a name to an IPv4 address and an AAAA record maps it to an IPv6 address.",
    "DHCP": "The Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol gives a device its network settings automatically when it joins a network. The exchange has four steps called DORA: the client broadcasts a Discover, a server replies with an Offer, the client sends a Request for the offered address, and the server confirms with an Acknowledgement. The lease contains an IP address, subnet mask, default gateway and DNS server, and it is valid for a limited lease time after which the client must renew it.",
    "NAT": "Network Address Translation lets many devices with private addresses share one public IP address. A NAT router rewrites the source address, and usually the source port, of outgoing packets and records the translation in a table. When the reply comes back, the router uses the table to rewrite the destination to the original private address and port. Private ranges such as 192.168.0.0/16 are not routed on the internet, and NAT also stops unsolicited incoming connections from reaching the private hosts.",
    "VLANs": "A virtual LAN splits one physical switch into several separate broadcast domains, so devices in different VLANs cannot talk to each other at layer 2 even when plugged into the same switch. Each VLAN is identified by a number, and a switch port is assigned to one VLAN as an access port. A trunk port carries the traffic of several VLANs between switches and tags each Ethernet frame with its VLAN ID using the 802.1Q standard. Traffic between two VLANs must pass through a router or a layer 3 switch.",
    "Hashing vs encryption": "Encryption is a two-way operation: data is turned into ciphertext with a key and can be turned back into the original with the right key. A hash function is a one-way operation: it maps any input to a fixed-length digest, the same input always gives the same digest, and the original cannot be recovered from the digest. Hashes are used to check integrity and to store passwords, ideally with a salt and a slow algorithm, while encryption is used to keep data confidential.",
    "Public-key cryptography": "Public-key cryptography uses a pair of mathematically linked keys: a public key that can be shared with anyone and a private key that its owner keeps secret. Data encrypted with the public key can only be decrypted with the private key, which gives confidentiality. A digital signature made with the private key can be checked with the public key, which proves who sent a message and that it was not changed. It is slower than symmetric encryption, so TLS uses it to agree on a shared symmetric key.",
    "Firewalls": "A firewall controls which network traffic is allowed to pass between networks, based on a list of rules. A packet filter checks the source and destination address, the port and the protocol of each packet. A stateful firewall also remembers existing connections, so that the replies to a connection started from inside are allowed back in automatically. Rules are checked in order and the first match decides, and a common design is to deny everything that is not explicitly allowed.",
    "Phishing": "Phishing is a social engineering attack in which the attacker pretends to be a trusted sender, usually by email, text message or a fake website, to trick the victim into giving away passwords, payment details or into running malware. Typical signs are an urgent request, a sender address that is slightly wrong, a link whose real destination differs from its text, and a request for credentials. Defences include checking the link before clicking, multi-factor authentication, email filtering and user training.",
    "Least privilege": "The principle of least privilege says that every user, program and process should have only the permissions it needs to do its job, and no more, for only as long as it needs them. It limits the damage when an account is compromised or a program has a bug. Examples are a normal user account for daily work instead of an administrator account, a database account that can only read the tables it needs, and temporary elevation for a single task.",
    "Processes vs threads": "A process is a running program with its own private memory address space and resources such as open files. A thread is a unit of execution inside a process; the threads of one process share its memory and open files, but each has its own stack and registers. Creating a thread and switching between threads is cheaper than for processes, and threads can share data easily, but a bug in one thread can corrupt the data of the others, and shared data needs locks. A crash in one process normally does not affect other processes.",
    "Virtual memory": "Virtual memory gives each process the illusion of its own large, continuous block of memory. The operating system and the memory management unit translate each virtual address into a physical address using a page table, working in fixed-size blocks called pages, commonly 4 KB. Pages that are not in RAM can be kept on disk in a swap file, and accessing one causes a page fault that makes the system load it back. This isolates processes from each other and lets programs use more memory than the machine has RAM, although heavy swapping makes the system slow.",
    "RAID": "RAID combines several physical disks into one logical volume for speed or for protection against disk failure. RAID 0 stripes data across disks, which is fast but has no redundancy, so one failed disk loses everything. RAID 1 mirrors the same data on two disks, so the usable capacity is that of one disk and the array survives one failure. RAID 5 stripes data with one disk's worth of parity spread over the disks: with 4 disks of 2 TB the usable capacity is 6 TB, and it survives one failed disk. RAID is not a backup.",
    "File permissions": "On Unix and Linux each file has permissions for three classes: the owner, the group and everyone else. Each class can have read (r), write (w) and execute (x) permission, which have the numeric values 4, 2 and 1, and the values are added up for each class. So rwxr-xr-- is 754: the owner has 7 (4+2+1), the group has 5 (4+1) and others have 4. The chmod command changes permissions and chown changes the owner. For a directory, execute permission means being allowed to enter it.",
    "Virtualization vs containers": "Virtualization uses a hypervisor to run several complete virtual machines on one physical host, and each virtual machine has its own guest operating system with its own kernel. A container packages an application with its libraries but shares the host operating system's kernel, using kernel features to isolate it from other containers. Containers are much lighter and start in seconds, while virtual machines give stronger isolation and can run a different operating system. Docker is a well-known container platform.",
    "Database normalization": "Normalization organizes a relational database so that each fact is stored in only one place, which avoids update, insert and delete anomalies. First normal form requires that every column holds a single value and that rows are unique. Second normal form requires that every non-key column depends on the whole primary key. Third normal form requires that non-key columns depend only on the key and not on other non-key columns. A customer's address, for example, belongs in a customers table and not repeated on every order row.",
    "Primary and foreign keys": "A primary key is a column or set of columns that uniquely identifies each row in a table; it cannot be null and cannot repeat. A foreign key is a column in one table that refers to the primary key of another table, which links the rows of the two tables. The database enforces referential integrity: it refuses to add a row whose foreign key value does not exist in the referenced table, and it controls what happens when the referenced row is deleted. For example orders.customer_id can be a foreign key to customers.id.",
    "Database indexes": "A database index is an extra data structure, usually a B-tree, that lets the database find rows by the value of a column without reading the whole table. Without an index a query on an unindexed column does a full table scan, which takes time proportional to the number of rows. With a B-tree index the lookup takes time proportional to the logarithm of the number of rows. Indexes make reads faster but use extra storage and slow down inserts, updates and deletes, because the index must be updated as well.",
    "Database transactions": "A transaction groups several database operations into one unit of work that either completes entirely or has no effect. Transactions follow the ACID properties: atomicity means all or nothing, consistency means the database moves from one valid state to another, isolation means concurrent transactions do not see each other's unfinished work, and durability means committed data survives a crash. A bank transfer, which subtracts from one account and adds to another, must be one transaction. COMMIT makes the changes permanent and ROLLBACK undoes them.",
    "Recursion": "Recursion is a technique in which a function solves a problem by calling itself on a smaller version of the same problem. Every recursive function needs a base case that stops the recursion and a recursive case that moves toward the base case. The factorial function is the classic example: factorial(0) is 1, and factorial(n) is n times factorial(n-1), so factorial(4) is 4 * 3 * 2 * 1 = 24. Each call uses space on the call stack, so very deep recursion can cause a stack overflow.",
    "Big-O notation": "Big-O notation describes how the running time or memory of an algorithm grows as the input size n grows, ignoring constant factors. Common classes from fastest to slowest growth are O(1) constant, O(log n) logarithmic, O(n) linear, O(n log n), O(n^2) quadratic and O(2^n) exponential. Searching an unsorted list of n items one by one is O(n), while binary search on a sorted list is O(log n): for one million items binary search needs at most about 20 comparisons. Nested loops over the same input are typically O(n^2).",
    "Pointers and references": "A pointer is a variable that stores the memory address of another value instead of the value itself. Following the pointer to reach the value is called dereferencing. A reference is a similar idea that acts as another name for an existing value, and in many languages it cannot be null or reassigned. Passing a pointer or reference to a function lets the function change the original value without copying it. A null pointer points to nothing, and a dangling pointer points to memory that has been freed, and using either is a common bug.",
    "APIs and REST": "An API is a defined way for one program to talk to another. A REST API exposes resources, each identified by a URL such as /users/42, and uses the standard HTTP methods on them: GET reads, POST creates, PUT or PATCH updates and DELETE removes. Each request is stateless, meaning the server keeps no memory of earlier requests and every request carries what it needs. Responses carry a status code, such as 200 for success, 404 when the resource is not found and 500 for a server error, and the data is usually JSON.",
}

assert set(NOTES) == set(TOPICS) and len(TOPICS) == 25
